{
  "slug": "byron-donalds",
  "name": "Byron Donalds",
  "summary": [
    {
      "text": "Byron Donalds won the November 3, 2026 election for governor of Florida, defeating David Jolly, according to unofficial results, and is set to take office when the new term begins.",
      "sources": [
        "s8",
        "s1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "He is the Republican U.S. representative for Florida's 19th District (since 2021) and served in the Florida House from 2016 to 2020; his running mate was former state Sen. Bryan Avila.",
      "sources": [
        "s1",
        "s2",
        "s3",
        "s4",
        "s5",
        "s6"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "paragraphs": [
    [
      {
        "text": "Donalds was born and raised in Crown Heights, Brooklyn, and earned a B.A. in finance and marketing from Florida State University in 2002.",
        "sources": [
          "s1",
          "s7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "He worked in finance, insurance and banking in Southwest Florida before entering politics.",
        "sources": [
          "s1",
          "s7"
        ]
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "text": "Donalds was elected to the Florida House of Representatives in 2016 and served District 80 until 2020.",
        "sources": [
          "s1",
          "s7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "He was then elected to the U.S. House in 2020 and re-elected in 2022 and 2024.",
        "sources": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "During the campaign, Donalds emphasized homeowners-insurance costs, healthcare price transparency, early-literacy and apprenticeship programs, requiring E-Verify, and cooperation with federal deportation efforts.",
        "sources": [
          "s1",
          "s2",
          "s3",
          "s4",
          "s5",
          "s6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "He lives in Naples.",
        "sources": [
          "s7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Byron Donalds won the November 3, 2026 general election for governor with 50% of the vote to David Jolly's 48.7%, according to unofficial results reported by NBC News.",
        "sources": [
          "s8"
        ]
      }
    ]
  ],
